## 3. How the interior is built (and why it looks over-engineered)

`render_html.py` → Chrome → `paginate.py` → `finalize.py`.

The pagination is deliberately **not** done in CSS. Chromium ignores
`break-before: left` entirely and treats page 1 as a left page, so
`break-before: right` puts chapter openers on **even** pages. Rather than fight
it, the HTML emits plain `page-break-before: always` and recto placement is
forced at the PDF layer, where real page indices are known.

To locate openers after rendering, each one carries an invisible marker
(`[[OP:ch-3]]`, white, 2pt, absolutely positioned). The build renders **twice** —
once with markers to learn the pagination, once without for the shipping file —
and asserts both raw renders have the same page count. Then blanks are inserted
before any opener that landed on an even page.

The blank pages this creates are not wasted: `finalize.py` stamps a pull-quote
from the upcoming chapter onto each one.

Table-of-contents page numbers come from the **rendered** PDF, which is why there
are three passes: render → read page numbers → re-render with numbers filled in.

