## 4. How the covers are built

<!-- REWRITE THIS SECTION PER BOOK. Record the actual
     decisions taken: which edge the front was cropped from and why, whether the
     back cover was scaled or redrawn, the photo crop coordinates, and anything
     that was wrong in the supplied art. A future agent will otherwise undo them. -->

**Never hand-calculate hardcover spine width.** KDP publishes the paperback
formula (`pages x caliper`) but deliberately does not publish one for hardcover.
`kdp_calculator.py` queries KDP's own Cover Calculator:

```bash
python3 build/kdp_calculator.py --binding hardcover --trim 6x9 --pages {PAGES} --paper cream
```

It caches to `.kdp_cover_cache.json` and self-checks that panels + spine
reconstruct the full cover width. `cover.py` drives every dimension from that
result, so a page-count change automatically produces correct geometry.

The **front cover** is the original art cropped to the panel aspect — the crop is
taken entirely off the *right* edge (blurred background) because the title block
lives on the left. This was checked visually against three candidate offsets; do
not recentre it.

The **back cover is redrawn from scratch** in `cover.py`, not scaled. The
original art was designed at 8.5 x 11; squeezed into 6 x 9 the body copy fell to
~7pt and collided with the barcode reserve. It is re-set in Oswald + Source Sans
against the sampled brand palette, reusing the real author photo cropped from
`back cover.png` at pixel coords `(556, 4012, 960, 4608)`.

The **spine was rebuilt**, not reused. `spine.png` in the original assets was a
mockup at the wrong aspect ratio, and its author name read "JULIE NEIL" — one L.
The rebuilt spine is spelled correctly and sizes its type from the available safe
band rather than a fixed guess.

