## 6. Common problems and what to change

**"The author edited the manuscript."**
Replace the .docx in `scripts/`, update `manuscript` in `book.json`, then rerun
`build_everything.sh`. **Check the new page count.** If it changed, the covers are
invalid — `cover.py` will pick up new geometry automatically, but you must rerun
it and rerun `package_kdp.py --pages <new count>`.

**"The chapter classification is wrong / a heading was treated as body text."**
`model.py` classifies by font size and boldness from the source .docx
(size 30 + bold + centred = chapter; 26 + bold = subhead). It also hardcodes
paragraph indices for the front and back matter (`FRONT_SKIP`, `GRATITUDE`,
`BACK_ONE_MORE`, `BACK_AUTHOR`). A different manuscript will need these adjusted.
Run `python3 build/model.py` and read the printed chapter table before trusting it.

**"The page count needs to change."**
Body type size and leading are in `render_html.py` (`font-size`, `line-height` on
`body`). This is how the book went from 34 to 100 pages: larger type, recto-only
chapter starts, a notes page per chapter, pull-quote pages. Do not pad with
filler a reader will notice.

**"KDP rejected the interior."**
Run `python3 build/verify.py <pdf> --expect-trim {TRIM_CODE} --expect-even`. It checks
trim, page parity, font embedding, Type3 fonts and recto openers. The usual
culprits are a non-embedded font (see §7) or a Type3 font from a missing glyph.

**"The spine text is off-centre / wrapping onto the front."**
Almost always paper colour. Cream is 0.0025 in/page, white 0.002252 — an 11%
difference. Confirm `paper` in `book.json` matches what is selected on KDP.

**"The back cover copy needs to change."**
`BACK_COPY` at the top of `build_back()` in `cover.py`. The layout is measured,
not absolute — it flows top-down with explicit gaps and the author box fills the
remaining space down to the barcode reserve. If you add copy, something else has
to shrink; the script prints the y-positions of the star divider and author box
so you can see what is running out of room.

**"The listing copy needs changing."**
`listing` in `scripts/book.json`, then rerun `write_listing.py`. Only use claims
that appear in the author's own manuscript or cover copy — descriptions are where
invented credentials creep in.

